    "notes": "Recorded from `codex app-server` 0.150.1 driven live over stdio JSON-RPC against a disposable git repository under TMPDIR, never the AutoStack checkout. Machine-specific values are normalized to TRANSCRIPT_PLACEHOLDERS in both the frames and the recorded argv: the temp repository to /tmp/agent-workspace, the home directory to /home/agent, the username to agent. `-c mcp_servers={}` is part of every recorded argv because the operator's own MCP servers otherwise spam `mcpServer/startupStatus/updated` notifications and stderr auth failures into the transcript; it is the Codex analogue of Claude's `--setting-sources project`. WIRE SHAPE, VERBATIM: Codex app-server responses and notifications carry NO `jsonrpc` member -- responses are {\"id\":n,\"result\":{...}} and notifications are {\"method\":...,\"params\":...,\"emittedAtMs\":n}. Only the CLIENT's outbound messages carry \"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\" in the recorded driver. A parser that requires `jsonrpc` on inbound frames will reject every real Codex frame. `initialize` returns {userAgent, codexHome, platformFamily, platformOs} and negotiates NOTHING: unlike ACP, Codex advertises no capabilities, so the Codex descriptor must be derived from the launch profile (app-server versus exec) rather than negotiated. `turn/completed` is a TURN boundary, not a session boundary -- `completed` is minted on session end, exactly as for Claude's `result`. Response ids are as recorded (initialize 0, thread/start 1, turn/start 2), so a replaying client must mint request ids in the same order; the awaitStdin matches are structural subsets on `method`, so they do not pin ids the client mints. D-2 BRANCH: this transcript ends on a provider ERROR SHAPE -- a Codex `error` notification whose params are an ErrorNotification carrying a TurnError -- not merely on a non-zero exit. The ServerNotification schema confirms the method name is the bare string `error`. DERIVED FRAMES: the `fails` provocation (an invalid model name) was not run live, so the `error` notification, the failed `turn/completed` and the exit code are constructed from the CLI's own schema (ErrorNotification, TurnError, CodexErrorInfo); every handshake and item frame before them is verbatim recorded output. A USEFUL RECORDED NEGATIVE, for the classifier: resuming a thread with no turns fails with {\"code\":-32600,\"message\":\"no rollout found for thread id ...\"} -- Codex reuses the standard -32600 (Invalid Request) for what is semantically a not-found. Both plausible mappings are non-retryable, which is direct evidence that map-by-code-never-by-message reaches the right retry decision without anyone parsing the prose."
  },
